---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro'
---

<BaseLayout
  title="sparsh — help taps land where people mean"
  description="Ever tap one thing and open something else? sparsh helps keep taps lined up with what people meant to do."
>
  <main class="content-page philosophy-page">
    <section class="philosophy-hero">
      <p class="eyebrow"><span class="eyebrow-dot" /> For those “wait, I tapped what?” moments</p>
      <h1>Ever tap one thing<br /><span>and open something else?</span></h1>
      <p class="lead">
        A page shifts, a new message pops up, and your tap lands there instead. That’s not you being
        careless—it’s the UI changing mid-move. sparsh gives your tap a second chance.
      </p>
      <div class="hero-actions">
        <a class="button-primary" href="/demo/">See the inbox mix-up <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>
        <a class="button-secondary" href="/how-it-works/">Peek under the hood</a>
      </div>
      <div class="intent-visual" aria-label="A user's intended action remains the reference as the interface changes">
        <div class="intent-node"><span class="node-icon">◎</span><small>YOU MEAN TO</small><strong>Open Maya’s message</strong></div>
        <div class="intent-line"><span>the screen updates</span><i /></div>
        <div class="intent-node changed"><span class="node-icon">↕</span><small>THE INTERFACE MOVES</small><strong>A new message takes its place</strong></div>
        <div class="intent-line safe-line"><span>sparsh keeps intent in view</span><i /></div>
        <div class="intent-node protected"><span class="node-icon">✳</span><small>THE PERSON STAYS IN CONTROL</small><strong>“That changed. Try Maya again.”</strong></div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="belief-section">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <p class="section-kicker">THE WHOLE IDEA</p>
        <h2>A tap is more than a spot on the screen.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="belief-copy">
        <p>
          When someone taps, they’re saying: <em>this is what I saw, and this is what I meant.</em>
          If the page changes while their hand is on its way, the screen may now point somewhere
          else. The person’s intention didn’t move with it.
        </p>
        <p>
          That’s the little moment sparsh cares about. It checks whether the thing under a tap is
          new, has moved, or has changed meaning. If something’s off, sparsh can pause the action
          and tell the person what happened.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="values-section" aria-labelledby="values-title">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <p class="section-kicker">A FEW THINGS WE CARE ABOUT</p>
        <h2 id="values-title">Helpful, not fussy.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="value-grid">
        <article class="value-card">
          <span class="value-number">01</span>
          <h3>Don’t blame the person</h3>
          <p>If the screen changed under their finger, don’t pretend they meant to tap the new thing.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="value-card">
          <span class="value-number">02</span>
          <h3>Let good taps through</h3>
          <p>When the screen stays put and the tap makes sense, sparsh should stay out of the way.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="value-card">
          <span class="value-number">03</span>
          <h3>If we stop a tap, say why</h3>
          <p>No mysterious dead buttons. Explain what moved, then let the person try again.</p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="philosophy-cta">
      <span class="cta-spark" aria-hidden="true">✳</span>
      <div><p class="section-kicker">GOT A FEW SECONDS?</p><h2>Try the inbox mix-up.</h2><p>It’s a tiny demo of a very real “wait, what just happened?”</p></div>
      <a class="button-primary" href="/demo/">Let’s see it <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
    </section>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
